## Answer: does MIMO's extra compute come at little cost on this CPU?

Partly. MIMO at rank 4 does 3.6x the FLOPs of SISO per step (25 vs 7 FLOP per state element) on the same 2 MiB state, so its arithmetic intensity rises from 0.85 to 2.90 FLOP/B. When the state must stream from DRAM, as it does for a deep model, the extra compute is mostly hidden behind the memory traffic: one AVX-512 core takes 196 µs (SISO) vs 257 µs (MIMO-4), 1.31x, and 8 cores take 41 vs 48 µs (1.18x), far below the 3.6x FLOP ratio. With AVX2 only, as on most laptops, the ratio grows to 1.58x on one core (209 vs 330 µs), because half-width vectors halve the compute headroom. When the state is cache-resident (8 threads, 256 KiB of state per core in L2), the step becomes compute-bound and MIMO-4 costs 1.80x (13 vs 24 µs). For reference, Mamba-2 with the same state takes 192 µs on one core (DRAM-resident). For a full layer, the Q4_K_M in_proj/out_proj reads (~14.7 MB for SISO, ~15.6 MB for MIMO-4, whose B/C projections are 4x wider) dominate: per layer, SISO and MIMO-4 come to about 1279 vs 1405 µs on 1 thread (+10%) and 200 vs 216 µs on 8 threads (+9%). So on this CPU MIMO's extra compute is not free inside the SSM kernel, but at the level of a decode step it costs on the order of 10%, and less when the kernel is memory-bound.

